[x] Make query output consistent across named/index and add-charter $Fixed: add action/add charter/delete action now emit VerbOutcome::Added/Deleted through verb_result::emit (JSON when piped, prose at a terminal), matching update/complete/cancel/reopen. Decided: CLI-only outcomes reuse the same VerbOutcome/transaction_result.schema.json rather than a separate schema (its own doc comment already claims full mirroring); added reopened/added/deleted to the schema's kind enum and already-open to VerbError, and closed the spec_conformance gap that quietly skipped Reopened. Investigated and left alone: query named (standard SPARQL Results JSON) vs query index (curated JSON-LD/NDJSON index contract) are deliberately different query families per specifications/query_output.md, not an inconsistency to unify.$ ^2026-08-23T15:07 %2026-09-17T20:54 #01a030aa-2e92-79c1-b4dc-a0ad2e058ff8
[x] Purge journaling text and fix link definitions in workspace spec $workspace.md's Mutation durability section described the exclusive OS lock + .pending journal protocol direct-delivery retired. Confirmed against clearhead-workspace-fs: no lock file, no journal exist in the current implementation. Rewrote the section to describe the real mechanism (EffectBatch + per-resource precondition, optimistic concurrency, additions-before-removals ordering) and fixed the archive section's matching 'journalled' claim. Also fixed the actual link-definitions bug: all reference-style definitions sat on one line so only the first parsed; split one per line and corrected the decisions/decision-28 targets to ../docs/DECISIONS.md (moved out of the platform root tonight). Also fixed four stale DECISIONS.md/SUBMODULES.md/WORKTREES.md links in platform's own README.md, found during the same sweep. specifications fe6881d, platform 8acb8b6.$ ^2026-09-15T01:24 %2026-09-17T21:59 #01a0a42b-3bf3-7423-a374-fc63bfec6d48
[x] Route charter markdown writes through EffectBatch delivery $jot, close and update call atomic_write directly and skip the precondition revision check. Confirmed 2026-09-17: this is NOT missing machinery to build from scratch — EffectBatch::preconditions + ResourcePrecondition/ExpectedResource::Revision + validate_preconditions + DeliveryError::Conflict/ResourceConflict already exist and are already enforced for action mutations (clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs). Charter markdown writes just never route through that seam; bring them onto the same EffectBatch+precondition path actions already use, don't build a second mechanism.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core 0f49f67: added `CharterDocument` plus `read_charter_document`/`write_charter_document` to clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs. The read captures the `.md` revision (ExpectedResource::Revision, or Missing for a not-yet-materialized charter) and the write delivers a single `Effect::Write` batch carrying that revision as its precondition through the same `deliver` → `validate_preconditions` path action mutations use — no second mechanism. Conflict reporting is still flattened to a string at that call site; that is 01a0b2c2, the next task. In clearhead-cli/src/commands/charter.rs: `jot` now derives both its base content and its created-vs-updated reporting from the document it actually read (std::fs::read_to_string and durability::atomic_write are gone from that verb); `update_charter` and `close_charter` read purely for the precondition and keep regenerating content from the loaded model, so their happy path is unchanged. `charter_document_location` mirrors `validate_source_path`: the path must be under `charters/` and end in `.md`. Tests added in the same file: round-trip write, creation of a missing document, stale-revision rejection (asserts the newer content survives), and the path guard. Verified end-to-end by building the CLI and running init → add charter → jot → update charter → close charter in a scratch workspace (`doctor` clean), plus the full pre-push gate. `add charter`'s two own atomic_write calls are deliberately left alone — different verb, not this action's scope.$ !3 ^2026-08-31T22:07 %2026-09-17T23:02 #01a05b5d-62be-73c0-8e70-154d78bd57a3
[x] Surface DeliveryError\:\:Conflict as a structured VerbError\:\:Conflict $Action mutations already carry a real precondition check (EffectBatch::preconditions, validate_preconditions in clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs) and already detect a stale-write race as DeliveryError::Conflict{conflict: ResourceConflict{path, expected, actual}}. But lib.rs:492 immediately flattens it via .to_string() into WorkspaceError::Actions(String), so every CLI verb loses the shape and an agent just sees an opaque error, not something it can branch on. Add VerbError::Conflict{path, expected, actual} (or similar) and thread the real ResourceConflict through instead of stringifying it. Found while designing the agent-surface MCP tools: multiple concurrent agents writing through capture/transact is exactly the scenario that turns this from theoretical into routine.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core 2aea96a: the conflict now travels as data at every layer that carries it. clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/mod.rs adds WorkspaceError::Conflict(ResourceConflict), and validate_preconditions (clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs) returns it instead of flattening via .to_string(). clearhead-core/src/verb_result.rs adds VerbError::Conflict{path, expected, actual} plus VerbError::from_workspace_error(&WorkspaceError); the module doc's "conflict joins later" note is corrected, since the write path has had compare-and-swap since the EffectBatch seam landed. clearhead-cli/src/main.rs searches the anyhow cause chain for a WorkspaceError, because commands wrap adapter errors in context, and emits {"kind":"conflict","path":...,"expected":...,"actual":...} on piped stdout like the other kinds. ResourceRevision, ExpectedResource and ResourceConflict gained Display for both the workspace error message and human stderr output. Tests: serialization of the new kind; projection from both a changed revision and a deletion underneath the write; the cause-chain walk behind anyhow context; and the workspace-fs seam test tightened from a message match to matches!(WorkspaceError::Conflict(_)). Deliberately not covered end-to-end at the CLI: producing a conflict requires real concurrency, so there is no external reproduction — the seam and the boundary are unit-tested instead. Full pre-push gate passed.$ !3 ^2026-09-17T21:25 %2026-09-17T23:09 #01a0b2c2-f791-733e-b323-6b16f3e7d24a
[x] Let add action create a missing charter actions file $promoting agent-surface failed with has no associated actions file; had to touch next.actions by hand. DEFERRED 2026-09-18: this is a clearhead-core CLI fix (add action's charter-actions-file-creation path), out of scope for the platform+specifications-only overnight pass. Not started.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core 4852414: the anchor is now derived, not demanded. clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/pathing.rs adds actions_anchor_for_document — the inverse of the loader's pairing rule (README.md → next.actions, otherwise stem ↔ stem) — re-exported through store/workspace mod. clearhead-cli/src/commands/action.rs resolve_acts_file (the only caller; add_action is its only user) falls back to it when the charter has no actions_file, so the location comes from the charter's document and never from its title. That exposed a second blocker: clearhead-workspace-fs validate_source_path canonicalized the target and so rejected a file that does not exist yet, giving "Invalid path"; it now keeps the exact previous canonical-file check for an existing target and, for a not-yet-created one, requires the deepest existing ancestor to be inside the charter tree. Delivery creates the file as part of the insert, so the first action and its anchor are a single atomic write. Tests: pathing unit tests for the inverse rule; a workspace-fs test that a missing in-tree anchor (and one in a not-yet-existing subdirectory) validates while an outside or non-.actions path is still rejected; two CLI integration tests (md-only charter, README.md collection charter). Verified end-to-end in scratch workspaces: both shapes create the right anchor and doctor stays clean. Other verbs (update/delete/complete/reopen) keep erroring on a charter with no actions file — they have nothing to act on, and add_action is resolve_acts_file's only caller.$ ^2026-09-14T17:09 %2026-09-17T23:13 <adopt-unified-root #01a0a266-01cf-73e3-acf2-a669be313387
[x] Stop deriving charter ids from titles in parse_charter $a charter md without frontmatter id gets a v5 id recomputed from its title on every load, so a retitle silently changes identity; the concept identity rules forbid live recompute. init sidesteps it via charter_frontmatter_id; loading should report the gap for doctor instead. DEFERRED 2026-09-18: this is a clearhead-core parser/doctor change, out of scope for the platform+specifications-only overnight pass. Not started.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core dd20473. parse_charter no longer derives an id from the title: specifications/workspace.md's Concept Identity section forbids recomputing an identity from mutable content, and the section's own answer for a missing anchor (the one the root clause states explicitly and workspace identity already uses) is an ephemeral id minted per load that loading never persists. clearhead-core/src/workspace/charter.rs now does `fm.id.unwrap_or_else(Uuid::now_v7)` with the rule documented at the function and in a new test (an id-less document's id is not v5(title) and differs between parses; a declared id survives a retitle). The gap is reported by the loader: crates/clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/assembly.rs adds the warning finding charter-document-without-id for every charter document that declares no id, naming the document (md_file, not its actions anchor) and pointing at the remedy — a minting write such as `clearhead update charter <it>`, or `clearhead init` for a new workspace. A charter whose sidecar records charter.id keeps adopting it (recorded truth beats a mint), and that variant's message points at the frontmatter. The root README keeps doctor's existing repair-aware root-readme-without-id, so no case is reported twice. Fixture fallout, all of it cases that pinned the forbidden derivation: three workspace snapshot fixtures (user-flat/charters/work.md, md-merge/charters/health.md, project-mixed/charters/health/README.md) now declare the ids their recorded .ron snapshots already expect, and the hand-written charters in seven archive_charter tests declare ids. Tests added: charter.rs (no title derivation, declared id survives retitle) and crates/clearhead-workspace-fs/tests/doctor.rs (the finding, both the no-sidecar and sidecar-carries-the-id variants). Observed on the real platform workspace: 8 findings — agent-surface and the seven someday/ charters declare no id — so doctor now exits 1 with 8 warnings there and each load prints them; declaring an id, or any minting write such as `clearhead update charter agent-surface`, clears it. NOT DONE, deliberately out of this action's scope: implicit_charter still seeds identity for a document-less charter with v5(name), which is likewise path-derived; it is a different case with its own documented rationale and tests, and the action scopes to parse_charter. Also not done: giving the model a way to distinguish a declared id from an ephemeral one downstream (so `show charter` can label it), which would need a new field on Charter/MarkdownCharter.$ ^2026-09-15T01:55 %2026-09-17T23:19 #01a0a447-8bcd-77b1-bda1-ef3201183590
